# A16 result

`STOP_CANDIDATE_EVENT_ENVELOPE_MISMATCH`. The single frozen candidate invocation created a private TCP-disabled Xvfb display, independently observed `a`, `d`, and Button1 down, SIGKILLed the process-owned input owner, confirmed it became a zombie, and retained a durable ledger with matching service-generation identity and all three active inputs. It then stopped before recovery because the candidate passed V39-style `{type, event}` wrappers while the outer recovery parser expected flat event objects. The stored RAW and ledger were independently audited with the corrected parser; it reconstructed exactly keys `a`/`d` and Button1. No emergency release was attempted or verified in A16. The session was killed during cleanup and Xvfb exited 0. This candidate allocation was not retried. The parser now accepts the known event envelope as well as a flat event for V39 integration; new runtime evidence requires a new allocation.
